This project builds on, adapts or credits the following work.

1. Elephant/goldfish method
   https://github.com/vshvedov/elephant-goldfish — MIT License.
   The P2 brainstorm (a sealed "elephant" take written first, then parallel
   context-free "goldfish" agents, each on one lens, then synthesis), the
   cold readiness check of each slice spec, and the brainstorm-elephant-goldfish
   capability skill are adapted from this method.

2. "Cranky Old Sam" reviewer persona — MIT License.
   The simplicity reviewer's voice (the blunt question "should this layer exist
   at all?") is adapted from the MIT-licensed "Cranky Old Sam" reviewer persona.

3. Contributed review profiles.
   - The code reviewer (shipyard:code-reviewer) is adapted from a contributed
     code-and-test review agent.
   - The security reviewer (shipyard:security-reviewer) and the security-audit
     capability skill are adapted from a contributed security-audit profile.

4. Books — ideas paraphrased, never quoted.
   The capability skills describe ideas from these books in their own words and
   cite them by author, title and topic. No text from them is reproduced.
   - John Ousterhout, "A Philosophy of Software Design" (complexity, deep
     modules, information hiding) — code-clarity.
   - Brendan Gregg, "Systems Performance" (methodologies such as USE,
     workload characterisation) — performance.
   - Eric Evans, "Domain-Driven Design" (ubiquitous language, bounded
     contexts, aggregates) — domain-design.

5. Earlier framework.
   Shipyard is a generic port of an earlier multi-agent delivery framework for
   a different command-line agent harness. See MIGRATION.md.

